why do we have different blood types  scientists estimate that blood types  have been around for at least 20 million  years the doctors have only known about  them for the last 116 years before 1900  countless people died after they were  injected with the wrong blood but what  makes a certain blood type wrong for one  person but right for another part of the  reason has to do with proteins called  antigens on the surface of a red blood  cells that define your blood type for  example type A blood contains type A  antigens whereas type B blood contains  type B antigens but if you mix blood  types a and B together you'll start an  all-out war in the body your blood  plasma contains antibodies that police  the body for anything foreign including  antigens that don't match your blood  type when you inject the wrong antigens  antibodies will attack by attaching to  them which causes the blood to clump  these clumps clog blood vessels which  disrupts circulation and can prove fatal  it only takes a few milliliters of the  wrong blood to cause a serious reaction  so it's critical that you know your  blood type there are 35 blood group  systems in the world but yours likely  falls under the two most common groups  the [ __ ] group and the RH group together  these groups include the eight blood  types that dominate over ninety percent  of the world's population odds are that  you're either a positive or a positive  which account for about 65 percent of  all human blood types  however blood type can vary from one  location to the next for example type O  blood is far more common in the Western  Hemisphere of all types a and B show up  more often in the eastern hemisphere in  India for instance your odds of being  born B positive are four times greater  than in the US yet your chances of  developing the rare a B negative blood  type are less than 2% worldwide  scientists are still trying to figure  out why there are so many different  blood types and where they came from  we know that blood types are not unique  to humans dogs cats horses and monkeys  to name a few  also have them but animals usually  contain different antigens than humans  so don't go injecting pets with your  blood there's also a growing body of  research revealing that blood types play  a role in our defense or lack thereof  against certain diseases  for instance people with blood type A  are at a higher risk for some forms of  pancreatic cancer and leukemia as well  as smallpox infections heart disease and  severe malaria whereas people with blood  type O are far less likely to develop  severe malaria but they're more prone to  ulcers and ruptured Achilles tendons so  the next time you give blood take a few  minutes to contemplate just how bizarre  business it is when that blood  eventually enters another human being  [Music]
 

